      "sequence_position": 12,
      "problem": "Prove that ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ is odd if $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}-2n+1 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{3}-2n+1={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ is {{field-7}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{3}-2n+1\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}-2n+1 & =(2a)^{3}-2(2a)+1 \\\\  & =8{a}^{3}-4a+1 \\\\  & =(8{a}^{3}-4a)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(4{a}^{3}-2a)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=4{a}^{3}-2a$ (highlighted above), we have that ${n}^{3}-2n+1=2b+1,$ which shows that ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=4{a}^{3}-2a.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}-2n+1 & =2{{(4{a}^{3}-2a)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ is odd.",
